From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 20:29:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: g16: Give every compute queue its own
 scheduling context

With g16_compute_queues above 1, the extra compute queues were made
under the first queue's scheduling context: they wrote no context image
of their own, and their queue images named the first queue's context.
With two queues the firmware faulted (address 0x20) soon after the
second queue's first commands.

macOS gives each compute queue its own scheduling context (its queue
info names a different one per queue) and shares only the compute
channel between its compute queues.

Make every compute queue the same way. cqueue::queue_set allocates one
queue's objects in a fixed order: queue info, state, ring, the two
stamp cells, scheduling context, notifier page, threshold and job list.
ComputeGraph::new uses it for queue 0 and for each further queue, and
ComputeGraph::init writes the context image before the notifier, the
job list and the queue image that names the context. Queue 0 keeps its
allocation order and objects, the render queues stay under queue 0's
context, and the extra queues are still allocated last.

The scratch the queue image names at +0x18 stays in the queue's own
queue info (+0xb0; macOS names its own +0x80). The queue ids stay 15 to
18: one per queue and consecutive, as macOS's 6, 7 and 8 are.

A host test checks the per-queue layout for one to four queues on the
real queue image. Every object belongs to its own queue. The image names
that queue's state, ring, job list, scratch and context, and its other
bytes are equal across queues. The ids are distinct and consecutive,
and their busy bits are clear of the render queues' bits. The old shared
context, any other shared object and bad ids all fail it.

g16_compute_queues stays 1 by default, so nothing changes at the
default.

Assisted-by: LLM
